		<string>19.0</string>
	</dict>
	<key>IOKitPersonalities</key>
	<dict>
		<key>IntelGtBringup</key>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key><string>local.nv3050.NV3050Probe</string>
			<key>IOClass</key><string>IntelGtBringup</string>
			<key>IOProviderClass</key><string>IOPCIDevice</string>
			<key>IOPCIMatch</key><string>0x9a688086</string>
			<key>IOMatchCategory</key><string>IntelGtBringup</string>
			<key>IOProbeScore</key><integer>500</integer>
		</dict>
		<key>NV3050Probe</key>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
			<string>local.nv3050.NV3050Probe</string>
			<key>IOClass</key>
			<string>NV3050Probe</string>
			<key>IOProviderClass</key>
			<string>IOPCIDevice</string>
			<!-- Confirmed against the target laptop on 2026-08-06:
			       01:00.0 3D controller [0302]
			       NVIDIA GA107M [GeForce RTX 3050 Mobile] [10de:25a2] rev a1

			     IOPCIMatch encodes (device << 16) | vendor, so 10de:25a2
			     becomes 0x25a210de. This is now an exact match rather than
			     the vendor-wide wildcard it started as. That wildcard would
			     have claimed any NVIDIA card, which matters if this kext ever
			     loads on a machine other than the target laptop.

			     probe() still rejects non-display functions as a second
			     filter; see NV3050Probe.cpp. Note the class here is 0x0302
			     (3D controller), NOT 0x0300 (VGA controller). That is the
			     muxless signature, and probe() checks base class 0x03 only,
			     so it accepts both.

			     Do not write a double hyphen anywhere in this comment. XML
			     forbids it inside comments, and plutil/plistlib reject the
			     whole file with a parse error that does not name the line. -->
			<key>IOPCIMatch</key>
			<string>0x25a210de</string>
			<!-- Outbid IOPCIFamily's generic driver, which would otherwise
			     claim the device before we get a chance. -->
			<key>IOProbeScore</key>
			<integer>100000</integer>
		</dict>
		<key>NVPeerMapperProbe</key>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
			<string>local.nv3050.NV3050Probe</string>
			<key>IOClass</key>
			<string>NVPeerMapperProbe</string>
			<key>IOProviderClass</key>
			<string>IOPCIDevice</string>
			<key>IOPCIMatch</key>
			<string>0x25a210de</string>
			<key>IOMatchCategory</key>
			<string>NVPeerMapperProbe</string>
			<key>IOProbeScore</key>
			<integer>99900</integer>
		</dict>
		<key>NVGspQueueObserver</key>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
			<string>local.nv3050.NV3050Probe</string>
			<key>IOClass</key>
			<string>NVGspQueueObserver</string>
			<key>IOProviderClass</key>
			<string>IOPCIDevice</string>
			<key>IOPCIMatch</key>
			<string>0x25a210de</string>
			<key>IOMatchCategory</key>
			<string>NVGspQueueObserver</string>
			<key>IOProbeScore</key>
			<integer>99800</integer>
		</dict>
		<key>NVGspQueueBackingService</key>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
			<string>local.nv3050.NV3050Probe</string>
			<key>IOClass</key>
			<string>NVGspQueueBackingService</string>
			<key>IOProviderClass</key>
			<string>IOPCIDevice</string>
			<key>IOPCIMatch</key>
			<string>0x25a210de</string>
			<key>IOMatchCategory</key>
			<string>NVGspQueueBackingService</string>
			<key>IOProbeScore</key>
			<integer>99700</integer>
		</dict>
		<key>IntelDisplayProbe</key>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
			<string>local.nv3050.NV3050Probe</string>
			<key>IOClass</key>
			<string>IntelDisplayProbe</string>
			<key>IOProviderClass</key>
			<string>IOPCIDevice</string>
			<!-- The Gen12 display track's personality. Same bundle as the
			     NVIDIA one on purpose: a bundle is the unit macOS loads, so
			     two bundles would mean two Info.plists, two signatures and
			     two kextload steps for what is one project. The
			     CFBundleIdentifier above must therefore be the BUNDLE's id,
			     not a per personality one.

			     0x9a688086 is (device << 16) | vendor for 8086:9A68, which
			     is the one Intel part this project has ever seen on real
			     silicon: the 2026-08-16 Linux boot on the target laptop
			     (igpu-results/tgl-capture-2026-08-16-022556.txt). Every
			     other TGL id in INTEL-DEVICE-IDS.md rests on the vendored
			     i915 table alone, so only the observed one is matched here.
			     tools/check-kext-personalities.py parses that document and
			     refuses any id it does not attribute to TGL.

			     probe() re reads the device id from config space and asks
			     IntelGpuId.hpp rather than trusting this string, because a
			     plist is data an operator can edit and nothing on the target
			     enforces the host side checker.

			     Do not write a double hyphen anywhere in this comment. XML
			     forbids it inside comments, and plutil/plistlib reject the
			     whole file with a parse error that does not name the line. -->
			<key>IOPCIMatch</key>
			<string>0x9a688086</string>
			<!-- Added 2026-08-20, when IntelFbPresentProbe became a SECOND
			     personality matching this same provider. Without a category on
			     BOTH, IOKit treats the provider's default category as exclusive
			     and exactly one of the two Intel personalities attaches, with
			     probe score deciding which. That failure is invisible in the way
			     this project cares about: the kext loads, one driver appears in
			     the IORegistry, and the missing one is indistinguishable from a
			     broken probe(). Setting it on only the new personality is the
			     shape that reads as fixed and is not, because a category is a
			     property of the personality that declares it. -->
			<key>IOMatchCategory</key>
			<string>IntelDisplayProbe</string>
			<!-- Lower than the NVIDIA personality's score, and deliberately
			     so: this shell only READS state firmware already programmed,
			     so it has no business outbidding a real display driver. It
			     still exceeds IOPCIFamily's generic score of 0. -->
			<key>IOProbeScore</key>
			<integer>1000</integer>
		</dict>
		<key>IntelSinkCandidateService</key>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
			<string>local.nv3050.NV3050Probe</string>
			<key>IOClass</key>
			<string>IntelSinkCandidateService</string>
			<key>IOProviderClass</key>
			<string>IOPCIDevice</string>
			<key>IOPCIMatch</key>
			<string>0x9a688086</string>
			<key>IOMatchCategory</key>
			<string>IntelSinkCandidateService</string>
			<key>IOProbeScore</key>
			<integer>800</integer>
		</dict>
		<key>IntelFbPresentProbe</key>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
			<string>local.nv3050.NV3050Probe</string>
			<key>IOClass</key>
			<string>IntelFbPresentProbe</string>
			<key>IOProviderClass</key>
			<string>IOPCIDevice</string>
			<!-- The Gen12 inherited framebuffer presenter, and the first
			     IOFramebuffer subclass in this bundle. Same bundle id as the
			     other two for the reason spelled above: a bundle is the unit
			     macOS loads.

			     Same observed device id as IntelDisplayProbe, 8086:9A68, and
			     for the same reason: it is the one Intel part this project has
			     ever seen on real silicon. Two personalities on one id is not a
			     conflict, because IOMatchCategory below puts them in separate
			     categories and IOKit will attach both.

			     IOMatchCategory is the load bearing key here and neither of the
			     other two personalities needs it. Without it IOKit treats the
			     provider's default category as exclusive, so exactly ONE of the
			     two Intel personalities attaches and which one wins is decided
			     by probe score. That failure is invisible in the way this
			     project cares about: the kext loads, one driver appears in
			     the IORegistry, and the missing one looks like a broken probe()
			     rather than a plist that never offered it a slot.

			     The score is below IntelDisplayProbe's on purpose. This class
			     ADOPTS a scanout rather than describing registers, so if only
			     one Intel driver can ever attach on a given machine the read
			     only reporter is the one worth having. It still exceeds
			     IOPCIFamily's generic score of 0.

			     Do not write a double hyphen anywhere in this comment. XML
			     forbids it inside comments, and plutil/plistlib reject the
			     whole file with a parse error that does not name the line. -->
			<key>IOPCIMatch</key>
			<string>0x9a688086</string>
			<key>IOMatchCategory</key>
			<string>IntelFbPresentProbe</string>
			<key>IOProbeScore</key>
			<integer>900</integer>
			<!-- IntelDisplayProbe carries a matching IOMatchCategory of its own.
			     Both are required: a category is a property of the personality
			     that declares it, so setting it on ONE of two personalities
			     sharing a provider leaves the other in the provider's default
			     category and back in the exclusive slot this key exists to
			     escape. One sided is the shape that reads as fixed and is not. -->
		</dict>
	</dict>
	<key>OSBundleRequired</key>
	<string>Local-Root</string>
</dict>
</plist>
